      solution: "Both cyclists start from the same point at the same moment and ride apart, so the gap between them is the sum of the two distances:\nd = v_A·t + v_B·t = (v_A + v_B)·t, so v_B = d/t − v_A.\nOnly now the numbers: d/t = 0.58 km ÷ (1/60) h = 34.8 km/h, so v_B = 34.8 − 20 = 14.8 km/h ≈ 4.11 m/s.\nWith the x-axis along cyclist A: v_Ax = +20 km/h, v_Bx = −14.8 km/h.",
      answer: "v_B = d/t − v_A = 14.8 km/h ≈ 4.11 m/s, opposite to cyclist A (v_Bx = −14.8 km/h)",
      meaning: "Assigning a positive direction to one cyclist turns \"opposite directions\" from a vague phrase into a hard number: the other velocity must be negative. Adding magnitudes to find the separation rate only works because they diverge.",
      whatIf: "What if they had ridden in the SAME direction, still 580 m apart after one minute? Now the gap grows by the difference of the speeds: d = (v_B − v_A)·t, so v_B = d/t + v_A = 34.8 + 20 = 54.8 km/h. One sign in the letter formula flips, and cyclist B must ride almost four times faster to open the same gap."
