      solution: "(a) Light moves at constant speed c, so S = c·t and t = S/c.\nt = S/c = 4.73·10¹⁶ ÷ 3.0·10⁸ ≈ 1.58·10⁸ s.\nIn years: 1 year ≈ 3.15·10⁷ s, so t ≈ 1.58·10⁸ ÷ 3.15·10⁷ ≈ 5.0 years.\n(b) Light needs 5.0 years to reach us, so the star is 5.0 light years away: S ≈ 5.0 ly. A light year is a distance, not a time.",
      answer: "(a) t = S/c ≈ 1.6·10⁸ s ≈ 5.0 years\n(b) S ≈ 5.0 ly",
      meaning: "Same d=v×t relationship as every problem in this anchor, applied to light instead of a train or a hiker — only the scale changes.",
      whatIf: "What if the light of another star takes 8.0 years to reach us — how far away is it in metres? S = c·t = 3.0·10⁸ · 8.0 · 3.15·10⁷ ≈ 7.6·10¹⁶ m, that is 8.0 ly."
