      concept: "Average speed formula applied to a circular orbit",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P35.svg", keyInfo: "circular orbit → trajectory: circle\nradius 2.0·10¹¹ m → r\nonce every 400 Earth days → T\nEstimate its orbital speed → find v\nin km/s → answer in km/s",
      statement: "A planet moves round its star in a circular orbit of radius 2.0·10¹¹ m and goes round once every 400 Earth days. Estimate its orbital speed in km/s.",
      solution: "In one orbit the planet travels one circumference, S = 2πr, in the time T:\nv = S/T = 2πr/T\nThe time in seconds:\nT = 400 days = 400·24·3600 s ≈ 3.456·10⁷ s (1 day = 24 h, 1 h = 3600 s)\nv = (2π · 2.0·10¹¹) ÷ (3.456·10⁷) ≈ 3.64·10⁴ m/s\nIn km/s: 3.64·10⁴ m/s = 36.4·10³ m/s = 36.4 **k**m/s (k = kilo = 10³)",
      answer: "v = 2πr/T ≈ 36 km/s",
      meaning: "Orbital speed is just average speed on a circle — the same S/t as for the jogger in P05, at a planetary scale. About 36 km every second: Moscow to Vladivostok in under four minutes.",
      whatIf: "What if the orbit were an ellipse — would the planet still have one orbital speed? No: it moves faster near the star and slower far from it. Then 2πr/T gives only its average speed over a whole orbit."
