      concept: "Same definition across wildly different scales; acceleration while slowing down",
      images: [], keyInfo: "Find the size of the average acceleration → find |a| = |Δv|/Δt\nspeeding up or slowing down → find: which\nfrom 0.60 m/s to 1.2 m/s → u, v\nover one minute → Δt = 1 min\nstarts from rest → u = 0\n2.0·10⁷ m/s → v\n4.0 ns → Δt\nslows from 90 m/s to 30 m/s → u, v\nover 20 minutes → Δt",
      statement: "Find the size of the average acceleration in each case, and say whether the body is speeding up or slowing down.\n(a) A man walking to the bus stop notices the time and picks up his pace from 0.60 m/s to 1.2 m/s over one minute.\n(b) In a hospital X-ray tube, an electron starts from rest and reaches 2.0·10⁷ m/s in 4.0 ns.\n(c) An airliner on its approach to the airport slows from 90 m/s to 30 m/s over 20 minutes.",
      solution: "The same recipe three times, always with the time in seconds:\na = Δv/Δt, where Δv = v − u\n(a) Δv = 1.2 − 0.60 = 0.60 m/s\nΔt = 1 min = 60 s\na = 0.60/60 = 0.010 m/s² — speeding up.\n(b) Δv = 2.0·10⁷ m/s\nΔt = 4.0 **n**s = 4.0·10⁻⁹ s (n = nano = 10⁻⁹ — the prefix table is in M01, section 7)\na = (2.0·10⁷) ÷ (4.0·10⁻⁹) = 5.0·10¹⁵ m/s² — speeding up.\n(c) Δv = 30 − 90 = −60 m/s: the speed went down by 60 m/s.\nΔt = 20 min = 20·60 s = 1200 s\n|a| = 60/1200 = 0.050 m/s² — slowing down: the acceleration points against the motion.",
      answer: "(a) |a| = |Δv|/Δt = 0.010 m/s², speeding up\n(b) |a| = 5.0·10¹⁵ m/s², speeding up\n(c) |a| = 0.050 m/s², slowing down",
      meaning: "Read each number as the card does — speed gained or lost every second. The walker adds one centimetre per second, every second. The airliner sheds five centimetres per second, every second — so slowly that nobody on board feels it. The electron gains five thousand million million metres per second, every second. One definition covers all three; only the powers of ten change.",
