      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P62.svg", solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P62.svg", keyInfo: "travelling at 30 m/s → u\nIt takes him 0.80 s to react → Δt_r, v = const\nslows down at 6.0 m/s² → |a|\nuntil it stops → v = 0\nHow far does the car travel while the driver is reacting → find S_r\nHow far does it travel while braking → find S_b\nWhat is the total stopping distance → find S = S_r + S_b\nWithout a full calculation → reason with the formulas\nhalf the speed → u/2",
      statement: "A car is travelling at 30 m/s on a motorway when the driver sees an emergency ahead. It takes him 0.80 s to react and press the brake. The car then slows down at 6.0 m/s² until it stops.\n(a) How far does the car travel while the driver is reacting?\n(b) How far does it travel while braking?\n(c) What is the total stopping distance?\n(d) Without a full calculation: if the car had been going at half the speed, by what factor would the distance in (a) shrink, and by what factor the distance in (b)?",
      solution: "(a) While he reacts, nothing changes the speed — constant velocity (T02):\nS_r = u·Δt_r = 30·0.80 = 24 m\n(b) Braking from u to v = 0; no time is given — the formula without t:\nv² = u² − 2|a|·S_b, so S_b = u²/(2|a|) = 30²/(2·6.0) = 900/12 = 75 m\n(c) S = S_r + S_b = 24 + 75 = 99 m\n(d) S_r = u·Δt_r grows like u: half the speed gives half the distance (×½).\nS_b = u²/(2|a|) grows like u²: half the speed gives a quarter of the distance (×¼).",
      answer: "(a) S_r = u·Δt_r = 24 m\n(b) S_b = u²/(2|a|) = 75 m\n(c) S = S_r + S_b = 99 m\n(d) reaction distance ×½, braking distance ×¼",
      meaning: "At 30 m/s, a car needs almost a football pitch to stop. At 15 m/s it needs 12 + 19 ≈ 31 m. Halving the speed cut the stopping distance by more than two thirds — because the braking part goes with the square of the speed.",
