      statement: "A cruise liner can slow down at no more than 0.0032 m/s². It is sailing at 10 knots (1 knot = 0.514 m/s) when the crew spots whales feeding directly ahead.\n(a) What is the shortest distance in which the ship can stop?\n(b) How long does stopping take?",
      solution: "First the speed in m/s: u = 10·0.514 = 5.14 m/s. The ship stops: v = 0.\n(a) No time is given — the formula without t:\nv² = u² − 2|a|·S, so S = u²/(2|a|)\nS = 5.14²/(2·0.0032) = 26.4/0.0064 ≈ 4100 m ≈ 4.1 **k**m\n(b) t = (u − v)/|a| = 5.14/0.0032 ≈ 1600 s ≈ 27 min (1 min = 60 s)",
