      concept: "Symbolic reasoning with door (3): speed at half the distance is v/√2, not v/2",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P64.svg", keyInfo: "starts from rest → u = 0\nstraight slope → trajectory: line\nconstant acceleration → a = const\na distance d → S = d\nher speed is v → v at the bottom\nhalfway down, at distance → S = d/2\nin terms of v → answer with v\nHow far from the start was she → find S\nwhen her speed was → speed v/2",
      statement: "A skier starts from rest and slides down a straight slope with constant acceleration. At the bottom, a distance d from the start, her speed is v.\n(a) What was her speed at the flag halfway down, at distance d/2? Give the answer in terms of v.\n(b) How far from the start was she when her speed was v/2?",
      solution: "From rest, the formula without t gives\nv² = 2a·S\nThe acceleration is the same all the way down, so the speed squared is proportional to the distance.\n(a) Bottom: v² = 2a·d. Halfway: v_h² = 2a·(d/2) = a·d = v²/2.\nSo v_h = v/√2 ≈ 0.71v\n(b) For the speed v/2: (v/2)² = 2a·S_q, and v² = 2a·d.\nDivide the first by the second: 1/4 = S_q/d, so S_q = d/4",
      answer: "(a) v_h = v/√2 ≈ 0.71v\n(b) S_q = d/4",
      meaning: "Halfway down she already has 71 % of her final speed. She reached half her final speed after only a quarter of the slope. Speed grows quickly at the start and more slowly later on, because speed squared, not speed, grows in step with distance.",
      whatIf: "What if you asked when, in time, she reached half her final speed? From rest v = a·t, so half the speed comes at exactly half the time. Since S = a·t²/2, half the time means a quarter of the distance — the same moment as in (b). Half the speed, half the time, a quarter of the slope."
