      concept: "Vertex and roots of S(t): maximum distance and return time",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P68.svg", solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P68.svg", keyInfo: "It leaves your hand at 3.0 m/s → u\nconstant acceleration of 1.8 m/s² → |a| = const\ndirected down the track → a points down\nPoint the x-axis up the track → + up the track\nWrite Sₓ(t) → find S_x(t)\nHow far up the track does the cart get → find S_x at the top\nAfter how long is it back in your hand → find t at S_x = 0",
      statement: "In the physics lab a cart is given a push up a long, low-friction tilted track. It leaves your hand at 3.0 m/s, and all the time it is on the track it has a constant acceleration of 1.8 m/s² directed down the track. Point the x-axis up the track.\n(a) Write Sₓ(t).\n(b) How far up the track does the cart get?\n(c) After how long is it back in your hand?",
      solution: "(a) u_x = +3.0 m/s, a_x = −1.8 m/s²:\nS_x = u_x·t + a_x·t²/2 = 3.0t − 0.9t²\n(b) The farthest point is where the cart stops for an instant: v_x = 3.0 − 1.8t = 0, so t = 3.0/1.8 ≈ 1.67 s.\nS_x = 3.0·1.67 − 0.9·1.67² ≈ 5.0 − 2.5 = 2.5 m\nThe formula without t gives the same: S = u²/(2|a|) = 3.0²/(2·1.8) = 9.0/3.6 = 2.5 m\n(c) Back in your hand means S_x = 0:\n3.0t − 0.9t² = 0, so t·(3.0 − 0.9t) = 0\nt = 0 is the push itself; the other root is t = 3.0/0.9 ≈ 3.3 s.",
      answer: "(a) S_x = 3.0t − 0.9t²\n(b) S = u²/(2|a|) = 2.5 m\n(c) t ≈ 3.3 s",
      meaning: "The return time, 3.3 s, is exactly twice the time to the top, 1.67 s. With the same acceleration all the way, the trip up and the trip down are mirror images — the parabola is symmetric about its vertex.",
      whatIf: "What if you asked how fast the cart is moving when it comes back to your hand? v_x = 3.0 − 1.8·3.33 = −3.0 m/s: the same speed it left with, now pointing down the track. The trip down is the mirror image of the trip up."
