      buildsOn: ["P69", "P71"], leadsTo: ["P73", "P80"],
      concept: "Meeting = equal coordinates; a parabola and a line can cross twice",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P72.svg", solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P72.svg", keyInfo: "straight bike path → trajectory: line\nstarts from rest at a painted line → u = 0, x_0 = 0\nspeeds up at 1.0 m/s² → a\nExactly 2.0 s later → the cyclist starts at t = 2.0 s\nriding at a steady 4.5 m/s → v = const\nin the same direction → same sign\nWrite x(t) for both → find x_1(t), x_2(t)\nWhen and where are they level with each other → find t, x with x_1 = x_2\nDescribe in words → explain",
      statement: "On a straight bike path, an e-scooter rider starts from rest at a painted line and speeds up at 1.0 m/s². Exactly 2.0 s later a cyclist crosses the same line, riding at a steady 4.5 m/s in the same direction.\n(a) Write x(t) for both, with the painted line at x = 0 and t = 0 when the scooter starts.\n(b) When and where are they level with each other?\n(c) Describe in words what happens.",
      solution: "(a) Scooter: x_1 = a·t²/2 = 0.5t²\nCyclist: she crosses x = 0 at t = 2.0 s, so for t ≥ 2.0 s: x_2 = 4.5·(t − 2.0)\n(b) Level means the same place at the same time, x_1 = x_2:\n0.5t² = 4.5·(t − 2.0), so t² − 9t + 18 = 0, (t − 3)(t − 6) = 0\nt = 3.0 s: x = 0.5·3.0² = 4.5 m\nt = 6.0 s: x = 0.5·6.0² = 18 m\n(c) At first the scooter is slow: the cyclist catches it at 3.0 s, 4.5 m past the line, and overtakes. But the scooter keeps speeding up; at t = 4.5 s it is already as fast as the cyclist (v = a·t = 4.5 m/s), and from then on it closes the gap. At 6.0 s, 18 m past the line, it draws level again and moves ahead for good.",
      answer: "(a) x_1 = 0.5t², x_2 = 4.5·(t − 2.0)\n(b) t = 3.0 s (x = 4.5 m) and t = 6.0 s (x = 18 m)\n(c) the cyclist overtakes at 3.0 s, the scooter overtakes back at 6.0 s",
      meaning: "Two roots, two overtakes. Section 8 of the card promised that crossing x(t) curves mean \"same place, same moment\". Here the parabola crosses the straight line twice, and each crossing is a real event on the path.",
      whatIf: "What if the cyclist rode at only 4.0 m/s — would she still overtake? The equation becomes t² − 8t + 16 = 0, with the single root t = 4.0 s: she just touches the scooter and never gets ahead. Any slower and there are no real roots — she never catches it at all."
