      statement: "A car doing a steady 21 m/s in a 50 km/h zone passes a parked patrol car. At that moment (t = 0) the patrol car sets off after it, accelerating at 4.0 m/s² for 8.0 s and then holding the speed it has reached.\n(a) When do the two cars have the same velocity? Has the patrol car caught up at that moment?\n(b) Is the patrol car level with the speeding car at t = 10 s?\n(c) When and where does it catch up?",
      solution: "Measure x from the parked patrol car.\nSpeeding car: x_1 = v_1·t = 21t\nPatrol car, stage 1 (0 to 8.0 s): x_2 = a·t²/2 = 2.0t², v_2 = a·t = 4.0t. At 8.0 s: v_2 = 32 m/s, x_2 = 128 m.\nStage 2 (after 8.0 s): x_2 = 128 + 32·(t − 8.0)\n(a) v_2 = v_1: 4.0t = 21, so t = 5.25 s.\nThen x_1 = 21·5.25 ≈ 110 m, x_2 = 2.0·5.25² ≈ 55 m — the patrol car is 55 m behind. Equal speed means only that the gap has stopped growing.\n(b) At 10 s: x_1 = 210 m; x_2 = 128 + 32·2.0 = 192 m. Not yet: 18 m behind.\n(c) The meeting must be in stage 2:\n128 + 32·(t − 8.0) = 21t, so 11t = 128, t ≈ 11.6 s\nx = 21·11.6 ≈ 244 m",
      answer: "(a) t = 5.25 s; no, about 55 m behind\n(b) no, 18 m behind\n(c) t ≈ 11.6 s, x ≈ 244 m from where it was parked",
