      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P77.svg", keyInfo: "At the same moment → same t = 0\nthrows a ball straight up at 20 m/s → u_1 = 20 m/s up\nstraight down at 20 m/s → u_2 = 20 m/s down\nTake g = 10 m/s² → g\nHow much later → find Δt\nyou are not told the height of the balcony → h unknown",
      statement: "Two friends stand on the same balcony. At the same moment one throws a ball straight up at 20 m/s and the other throws an identical ball straight down at 20 m/s. Take g = 10 m/s².\nHow much later than the second ball does the first ball land? Notice that you are not told the height of the balcony.",
      solution: "Follow the first ball. It rises until its velocity is zero: t_up = u_1/g = 20/10 = 2.0 s. Coming down, it needs another 2.0 s to get back to the balcony — and there it moves at 20 m/s downwards, exactly like the second ball when it was thrown.\nFrom that moment the two balls do exactly the same thing, so they need the same time to reach the ground, whatever the height.\nThe difference is the extra trip up and back:\nΔt = 2u_1/g = 2·20/10 = 4.0 s",
      answer: "Δt = 2u_1/g = 4.0 s, whatever the height of the balcony",
      meaning: "The height is not given because it does not matter. The two motions are identical from the balcony down, and the only difference is the 4.0 s detour the first ball makes. In formula form the gap is 2u/g.",
      whatIf: "What if both balls were thrown at 30 m/s instead — how big would the gap be? Δt = 2·30/10 = 6.0 s. The gap grows in proportion to the throwing speed."
