      buildsOn: ["P47", "P78"], leadsTo: ["P80", "P95"],
      concept: "Two moments with the same speed; velocity vs speed",
      images: [], solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P79.svg", keyInfo: "thrown straight up at 22 m/s → u_y = +22 m/s\nTake g = 9.8 m/s² → g\npoint the y-axis up → + up\nAt what moments → find t\na speed of 10 m/s → |v_y| = 10 m/s\nWhy are there two answers → explain\nvelocity equal to +10 m/s → v_y = +10 m/s\nHow high is the stone at each of those moments → find S_y at both moments",
      statement: "A stone is thrown straight up at 22 m/s. Take g = 9.8 m/s² and point the y-axis up.\n(a) At what moments is the stone moving at a speed of 10 m/s?\n(b) Why are there two answers? At which of them is its velocity equal to +10 m/s?\n(c) How high is the stone at each of those moments?",
      solution: "v_y(t) = u_y − g·t = 22 − 9.8t\n(a) A speed of 10 m/s means v_y = +10 (going up) or v_y = −10 (coming down).\nv_y = +10: t = (22 − 10)/9.8 ≈ 1.2 s\nv_y = −10: t = (22 + 10)/9.8 ≈ 3.3 s\n(b) The stone passes 10 m/s once on the way up and once on the way down. Only at t ≈ 1.2 s is the velocity +10 m/s; at 3.3 s it is −10 m/s.\n(c) No time is needed for the height — the formula without t:\nv_y² = u_y² − 2g·S_y, so S_y = (u_y² − v_y²)/(2g) = (22² − 10²)/(2·9.8) = 384/19.6 ≈ 20 m\nThe same at both moments — v_y² is the same.",
      answer: "(a) t ≈ 1.2 s and t ≈ 3.3 s\n(b) the same speed up and down; v_y = +10 m/s only at 1.2 s\n(c) S_y = (u_y² − v_y²)/(2g) ≈ 20 m both times",
      meaning: "Speed alone does not say which way the stone is going; the sign of vy does. At the same height the stone always has the same speed, once going up and once coming down. That is the symmetry of P77 at every height, not just at the start.",
      whatIf: "What if you asked when the stone moves at 30 m/s — how many moments now? Only one: v_y = −30 at t = 52/9.8 ≈ 5.3 s, on the way down, below the starting point (if there is room to fall). On the way up it was never faster than 22 m/s."
