      anchors: ["T04#vertical-motion"],
      buildsOn: ["P72", "P79"], leadsTo: ["P81", "P86"],
      concept: "Gap between two falling bodies grows linearly: constant relative velocity",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P80.svg", keyInfo: "one drop every 1.0 s → Δt = 1.0 s between drops\nIgnore air resistance → a = g\ng = 9.8 m/s² → g\nHow far apart are two neighbouring drops → find the gap\n2.0 s after the second of them breaks off → t = 2.0 s\nFind a formula for the gap → find gap(t)\nHow does the gap change → explain\nExplain the result by comparing the velocities → compare v_1, v_2",
      statement: "On a sunny spring day an icicle on a high roof drips one drop every 1.0 s. Ignore air resistance and take g = 9.8 m/s².\n(a) How far apart are two neighbouring drops 2.0 s after the second of them breaks off?\n(b) Find a formula for the gap between them a time t after the second drop breaks off. How does the gap change as they fall?\n(c) Explain the result by comparing the velocities of the two drops.",
      solution: "Let t be the time since the second drop broke off; the first drop has been falling for t + Δt.\n(a) First drop: S_1 = g·(t + Δt)²/2 = 9.8·3.0²/2 = 44.1 m\nSecond drop: S_2 = g·t²/2 = 9.8·2.0²/2 = 19.6 m\nGap = S_1 − S_2 ≈ 24.5 m\n(b) gap = g·(t + 1)²/2 − g·t²/2 = g·(2t + 1)/2 = 9.8t + 4.9 (metres, t in seconds)\nThe gap grows by 9.8 m every second — steadily.\n(c) The first drop has always been falling 1.0 s longer, so it is always faster by g·Δt = 9.8 m/s. A constant difference in velocity makes the gap grow at a constant rate.",
      answer: "(a) gap = S_1 − S_2 ≈ 24.5 m\n(b) gap = 9.8t + 4.9 m — it grows by 9.8 m every second\n(c) v_1 − v_2 = g·Δt = 9.8 m/s, always",
      meaning: "Each drop on its own speeds up, but relative to each other the drops move at a constant 9.8 m/s. Seen from one drop, the other moves away at constant speed. The g in each drop's motion cancels out when you subtract.",
      whatIf: "What if you photographed a whole column of drops at one moment — how would the gaps between them look? Each gap is 9.8 m longer than the one above it: spaced like 1, 3, 5, 7… A dripping tap lit by a strobe light shows exactly this."
