      statement: "A school model rocket lifts off from the ground with a constant upward acceleration of 3.0 m/s². Its fuel runs out at a height of 85 m, and from then on it moves freely. Take g = 9.8 m/s² and ignore air resistance.\n(a) How fast is the rocket moving when the fuel runs out?\n(b) What is the greatest height it reaches?\n(c) Sketch vy(t) from lift-off to the top.\n(d) How long does it take to fall from the top back to the ground?",
      solution: "(a) Stage 1, powered: u = 0, a = 3.0 m/s², S = h_1 = 85 m; no time — the formula without t:\nv_1² = 2a·h_1 = 2·3.0·85 = 510, so v_1 ≈ 22.6 m/s\n(b) Stage 2, free: it starts at 85 m moving up at v_1 and slows at g.\nΔh = v_1²/(2g) = 510/(2·9.8) ≈ 26 m\nH = h_1 + Δh ≈ 85 + 26 = 111 m\n(c) Stage 1 lasts Δt_1 = v_1/a = 22.6/3.0 ≈ 7.5 s: a gentle line rising from 0 to 22.6 m/s.\nStage 2 lasts Δt_2 = v_1/g = 22.6/9.8 ≈ 2.3 s: a steep line falling from 22.6 m/s to 0.\nThe peak of v_y(t) is where the fuel ran out, not where the rocket is highest.\n(d) From rest at H: t_fall = √(2H/g) = √(2·111/9.8) ≈ 4.8 s",
