      statement: "A football is kicked from the ground at 20 m/s, 50° above the horizontal. Ignore air resistance and take g = 9.8 m/s².\n(a) Split the launch velocity into its horizontal and vertical components.\n(b) Write x(t) and y(t).\n(c) Sketch x(t) and y(t), one under the other on the same time axis, until the ball lands. Mark the moment it lands and the moment it is highest.",
      solution: "(a) The components are the sides of the velocity triangle:\nu_x = u·cos α = 20·cos 50° ≈ 12.9 m/s\nu_y = u·sin α = 20·sin 50° ≈ 15.3 m/s\n(b) Horizontally nothing acts — constant velocity (T02): x = u_x·t = 12.9t\nVertically only g acts (T03 with a = −g): y = u_y·t − g·t²/2 = 15.3t − 4.9t²\n(c) Highest point: v_y = u_y − g·t = 0, so t_top = u_y/g = 15.3/9.8 ≈ 1.56 s, where y = 15.3·1.56 − 4.9·1.56² ≈ 12 m.\nLanding: y = 0 again at t = 2t_top ≈ 3.1 s, where x = 12.9·3.1 ≈ 40 m.\nx(t): a straight line up to about 40 m at 3.1 s. y(t): a parabola up to 12 m at 1.56 s and back to 0 at 3.1 s.",
      answer: "(a) u_x = u·cos α ≈ 12.9 m/s, u_y = u·sin α ≈ 15.3 m/s\n(b) x = 12.9t, y = 15.3t − 4.9t²\n(c) x(t): a straight line to ≈ 40 m; y(t): a parabola, top ≈ 12 m at t_top ≈ 1.56 s, lands at ≈ 3.1 s",
