      anchors: ["T04#independent-motions"],
      buildsOn: ["P84"], leadsTo: ["P86"],
      concept: "Same maximum height ⇒ same vertical motion ⇒ same flight time, whatever the range",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P85.svg", keyInfo: "from the same spot on level ground → same start, land at launch height\nexactly the same greatest height → H_A = H_B\nball A lands twice as far away as ball B → R_A = 2R_B\nIgnore air resistance → a_x = 0, a_y = −g\nWhich ball is in the air longer → compare t\nCompare their vertical launch components → compare u_y\ntheir horizontal launch components → compare u_x\nWhich ball was kicked faster → compare u\nWhich is moving faster at the top → compare v at the top",
      statement: "Two footballs are kicked from the same spot on level ground. Both reach exactly the same greatest height, but ball A lands twice as far away as ball B. Ignore air resistance.\n(a) Which ball is in the air longer?\n(b) Compare their vertical launch components, and their horizontal launch components.\n(c) Which ball was kicked faster? Which is moving faster at the top of its flight?",
      solution: "(a) The greatest height depends only on the vertical motion: H = u_y²/(2g). The same H means the same u_y, so the vertical motions are identical — same time up, same time down. Both are in the air for the same time.\n(b) Vertical components: equal. Same time in the air, but A goes twice as far, so u_x of A is twice u_x of B (R = u_x·t).\n(c) u = √(u_x² + u_y²): the same u_y, a bigger u_x, so A was kicked faster. At the top v_y = 0 and only u_x is left, so A is also faster at the top — twice as fast.",
      answer: "(a) the same time\n(b) u_y equal; u_x of A is twice u_x of B\n(c) A in both cases",
      meaning: "Looking at the two paths, A's long flat arc seems to take longer. It does not. Time in the air belongs to the vertical motion only; the horizontal motion just decides how far the ball gets in that time.",
      whatIf: "What if both balls were kicked at the same speed but at 30° and 60° — what would be the same now? The range (T04, section 7) — but the heights and flight times differ. The same range does not mean the same time."
