      statement: "During a flood, a rescue team fires a light line-carrying projectile from ground level to the flat roof of a building where people are waiting. The roof is 150 m high and its edge is 200 m away horizontally. The projectile lands on the edge of the roof 8.0 s after launch. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance.\n(a) Find the horizontal and vertical components of the launch velocity.\n(b) Find the launch speed and angle.\n(c) Is the projectile still rising or already falling when it lands on the roof?",
      solution: "(a) Horizontally: d = u_x·t, so u_x = d/t = 200/8.0 = 25 m/s\nVertically: h = u_y·t − g·t²/2, so u_y = (h + g·t²/2)/t = (150 + 5.0·8.0²)/8.0 = 470/8.0 ≈ 58.8 m/s\n(b) u = √(u_x² + u_y²) = √(25² + 58.8²) ≈ 64 m/s\ntan α = u_y/u_x = 58.8/25, so α ≈ 67° above the horizontal\n(c) v_y = u_y − g·t = 58.8 − 10·8.0 ≈ −21 m/s: already falling. It passed its top at t = u_y/g ≈ 5.9 s and came down onto the roof.",
      answer: "(a) u_x = d/t = 25 m/s, u_y = (h + g·t²/2)/t ≈ 59 m/s\n(b) u = √(u_x² + u_y²) ≈ 64 m/s at α ≈ 67°\n(c) falling: v_y ≈ −21 m/s",
      meaning: "The team did not aim at the roof edge; they aimed far above it, at 67°. The projectile climbs to about 173 m and drops onto the roof from above. Landing on a roof needs a falling projectile, otherwise it would hit the wall.",
      whatIf: "What if the team wanted it to land in 6.0 s instead — would it still come down onto the roof? u_x = 200/6.0 ≈ 33 m/s, u_y = (150 + 180)/6.0 = 55 m/s, and at landing v_y = 55 − 60 = −5 m/s. It only just falls onto the roof — a faster, flatter shot, close to the limit."
