      statement: "On a flat beach a girl throws a ball at 20 m/s, 30° above the horizontal, from (roughly) ground level. Take g = 10 m/s² and ignore air resistance.\n(a) How long does the ball take to reach its highest point?\n(b) How high does it go?\n(c) How far away does it land?",
      solution: "Split the launch velocity: u_x = u·cos α = 20·cos 30° ≈ 17.3 m/s, u_y = u·sin α = 20·sin 30° = 10 m/s.\n(a) At the top v_y = 0: v_y = u_y − g·t, so t_top = u_y/g = 10/10 = 1.0 s\n(b) The formula without t for the vertical motion: 0 = u_y² − 2g·H, so H = u_y²/(2g) = 100/20 = 5.0 m\n(c) On level ground the way down mirrors the way up: the flight lasts 2t_top = 2.0 s. Horizontally the ball keeps u_x all that time:\nR = u_x·2t_top = 17.3·2.0 ≈ 35 m",
