      concept: "Reconstructing the launch from the top of the flight",
      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P96.svg", solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P96.svg", keyInfo: "level football pitch → lands at launch height\nreaches its highest point → v_y = 0\n0.90 s after the kick → t_top\n16 m (horizontally) → x_top\ng = 9.8 m/s² → g\nignore air resistance → v_x = const\nthe vertical component of the launch velocity → find u_y\nthe horizontal component → find u_x\nthe launch speed and angle → find u, α\nthe greatest height and the total range → find H, R",
      statement: "A video of a long kick on a level football pitch shows that the ball reaches its highest point 0.90 s after the kick, when it is 16 m (horizontally) from where it was kicked. Take g = 9.8 m/s² and ignore air resistance.\nFind:\n(a) the vertical component of the launch velocity;\n(b) the horizontal component;\n(c) the launch speed and angle;\n(d) the greatest height and the total range.",
      solution: "Split the motion into two. Horizontally nothing pushes or pulls the ball (no air resistance), so v_x stays constant. Vertically gravity slows the ball steadily: this is uniformly accelerated motion with a_y = −g (y-axis up). At the top v_y = 0.\n(a) Take the UAM formula v = u + a·t and write it for the y-direction: v_y = u_y − g·t.\nAt the top v_y = 0 and t = t_top: 0 = u_y − g·t_top, so u_y = g·t_top = 9.8·0.90 ≈ 8.8 m/s.\n(b) Horizontally x_top = v_x·t_top, so v_x = x_top/t_top = 16/0.90 ≈ 17.8 m/s.\n(c) u = √(v_x² + u_y²) = √(17.8² + 8.8²) ≈ 20 m/s; tan α = u_y/v_x = 8.8/17.8, so α ≈ 26°.\n(d) H is the vertical displacement from the kick to the top. Take the UAM formula v² = u² + 2a·S and write it for the y-direction:\nv_y² = u_y² + 2·(−g)·H.\nAt the top v_y = 0: 0 = u_y² − 2g·H, so H = u_y²/(2g) = 8.8²/(2·9.8) ≈ 4.0 m.\nCheck with another UAM formula, S = u·t + a·t²/2: H = u_y·t_top − g·t_top²/2 = 8.8·0.90 − 9.8·0.90²/2 ≈ 4.0 m. ✓\nOn level ground the flight is symmetric: the way down mirrors the way up, so R = 2·x_top = 2·16 = 32 m (total time 2·t_top = 1.8 s).",
      answer: "(a) u_y = g·t_top ≈ 8.8 m/s\n(b) v_x = x_top/t_top ≈ 17.8 m/s\n(c) u = √(v_x² + u_y²) ≈ 20 m/s at α ≈ 26°\n(d) H = u_y²/(2g) ≈ 4.0 m, R = 2·x_top = 32 m",
      meaning: "A single frame — the top — holds the whole kick. The time to the top gives uy, the distance gives ux, and symmetry doubles everything for the landing.",
      whatIf: "What if the ball had come down on a hill, higher than the pitch? The symmetry breaks: the ball meets the hill before it gets back to pitch level, so it lands earlier than 2·t_top = 1.8 s and closer than 2·x_top = 32 m. R = 2·x_top belongs to level ground only."
