      solution: "(a) The top of the flight must be at h_w: H = u_y²/(2g) = h_w, so u_y = √(2g·h_w) = √(2·10·5.0) = 10 m/s\nsin α = u_y/u = 10/20 = 0.5, so α = 30°\n(b) The top is reached after t_top = u_y/g = 1.0 s. In that time the ball moves d = u_x·t_top = u·cos α·t_top = 20·cos 30°·1.0 ≈ 17 m horizontally. Stand about 17 m from the wall.\n(c) The highest any throw at 20 m/s can go is straight up: H_max = u²/(2g) = 20²/(2·10) = 20 m. A 25 m wall is out of reach at any angle.",
      answer: "(a) sin α = √(2g·h_w)/u, α = 30°\n(b) d = u·cos α·t_top ≈ 17 m\n(c) no: even straight up the ball reaches only H_max = u²/(2g) = 20 m",
      meaning: "The height of the wall picks uy, and uy together with your speed picks the angle. Only then does the horizontal motion decide where you stand. The order of the reasoning follows the order of the physics: vertical first, horizontal second.",
