      images: [], sketch: "cards/sketches/P106.svg", solutionFig: "cards/solutions/P106.svg", keyInfo: "the 100 m bend → S = 100 m\na half-circle → turns through 180°\nat a constant 8.0 m/s → |v| = const\nExplain why she is still accelerating → explain\nCompare her velocity as she enters the bend with her velocity as she leaves it → compare v_in, v_out\nFind her average acceleration over the bend → find a_avg\nits size and its direction → size and direction",
      statement: "A runner goes round the 100 m bend of a 400 m track — a half-circle — at a constant 8.0 m/s.\n(a) Her speed does not change. Explain why she is still accelerating.\n(b) Compare her velocity as she enters the bend with her velocity as she leaves it.\n(c) Find her average acceleration over the bend: its size and its direction.",
      solution: "(a) Velocity has a size and a direction. On the bend the direction keeps turning, so the velocity keeps changing — and any change of velocity is an acceleration, even when the speed stays the same.\n(b) She enters the bend running, say, east at 8.0 m/s, and leaves it running west at 8.0 m/s: the same speed, opposite directions.\n(c) Take the x-axis east. v_x goes from +8.0 to −8.0 m/s:\nΔv_x = −8.0 − 8.0 = −16 m/s\nThe bend takes Δt = S/|v| = 100/8.0 = 12.5 s.\n|a_avg| = |Δv_x|/Δt = 16/12.5 ≈ 1.3 m/s², pointing west — across the bend, towards its inside.",
