      statement: "A child is on a playground swing hanging on ropes 2.0 m long. An accelerometer in her pocket shows that, at the lowest point of the swing, her acceleration is 12.5 m/s², pointing straight up.\n(a) Why is the acceleration at the lowest point directed exactly towards the centre (the top bar)?\n(b) How fast is she moving at the lowest point?",
      solution: "On a curved path the acceleration has two parts: the centripetal part a_c = v²/r, always pointing to the centre (it turns the velocity), and the tangential part a_t, along the path (it changes the speed).\n(a) Just before the lowest point she is speeding up (a_t forward); just after it she is slowing down (a_t backward). At the lowest point itself the speed is at its maximum and is not changing for an instant, so a_t = 0.\nOnly the centripetal part is left: a = a_c, pointing to the centre of the circle — straight up to the bar.\n(b) The whole acceleration is centripetal: a = a_c = v²/r, so v² = a·r and v = √(a·r).\nOnly now the numbers: v = √(12.5·2.0) = √25 = 5.0 m/s.",
      answer: "(a) at the lowest point the speed is at its maximum, so a_t = 0 and only a_c is left, pointing to the bar\n(b) v = √(a·r) = 5.0 m/s",
      meaning: "5 m/s is a brisk swing (18 km/h). At the bottom she feels heavier, because her velocity is turning upward. At the top of each swing she stops for an instant, the centripetal part is zero, and she feels light.",
      whatIf: "What if the accelerometer were read halfway up the swing instead? There she is both turning and slowing down: the acceleration has a centripetal part (towards the bar) and a tangential part (back along the path), so it no longer points at the bar, and v = √(a·r) would give a wrong speed. The bottom is the only point where the whole acceleration is v²/r."
