      statement: "A trolley moves along a long straight track. The graph shows how its velocity varies with time. Describe how the acceleration of the trolley varies with time. Your answer should include calculations.",
      solution: "The acceleration is the slope of v(t). The graph is two straight pieces, so on each piece a is constant.\nFrom 0 to 8 s: v goes from 4 m/s to 16 m/s.\na_1 = Δv/Δt = (16 − 4)/8 = 1.5 m/s²\nFrom 8 s to 20 s: v goes from 16 m/s to −20 m/s.\nΔv = −20 − 16 = −36 m/s\na_2 = Δv/Δt = −36/12 = −3.0 m/s²\nIn words: for the first 8 s the acceleration is constant, +1.5 m/s². At t = 8 s it changes suddenly to −3.0 m/s² — twice as big and in the opposite direction — and stays constant until 20 s. It does not change when the velocity passes through zero: the trolley stops for an instant and comes back with the same acceleration.",
      answer: "a_1 = Δv/Δt = +1.5 m/s² (0–8 s), then a_2 = −3.0 m/s² (8–20 s), constant on each piece",
