      concept: "Internal energy of a monatomic ideal gas from n and T",
      images: [], keyInfo: "0.25 mol → n\n300 K → T₁\nmonatomic ideal gas → U = 3/2·nRT\nCalculate the internal energy of the helium → find U₁\ncools to 280 K → T₂\nCalculate the change in its internal energy → find ΔU (sign!)",
      statement: "A party balloon holds 0.25 mol of helium at a temperature of 300 K. Helium behaves as a monatomic ideal gas.\n(a) Calculate the internal energy of the helium.\n(b) The balloon is carried outside and the helium cools to 280 K. Calculate the change in its internal energy.",
      solution: "(a) For a monatomic ideal gas all the internal energy is kinetic energy of the atoms: U = 3/2 · n·R·T (B4.1, section 1).\nU_1 = 3/2 · n·R·T_1 = 1.5 · 0.25 · 8.31 · 300 ≈ 935 J\n(b) n and R do not change, only T does: ΔU = 3/2 · n·R·ΔT, where ΔT = T_2 − T_1 = 280 − 300 = −20 K.\nΔU = 1.5 · 0.25 · 8.31 · (−20) ≈ −62 J\nThe minus sign says the internal energy went down — the gas got colder.",
      answer: "(a) U_1 = 3/2 · n·R·T_1 ≈ 935 J\n(b) ΔU = 3/2 · n·R·ΔT ≈ −62 J",
      meaning: "Almost a kilojoule is stored in a toy balloon — as the random motion of helium atoms. Cooling by 20 K took away about 7 % of it, exactly the fraction by which the kelvin temperature dropped (20 out of 300).",
      whatIf: "What if the balloon held twice as much helium at the same temperatures? Both answers double: U and ΔU are proportional to n. The temperature tells you the energy per atom; the amount tells you how many atoms share it."
