      statement: "Mira heats a sealed flask of an ideal gas from 20 °C to 80 °C. She says: \"The temperature is four times higher, so the internal energy of the gas is four times larger.\"\nState and explain whether Mira is right.",
      solution: "U = 3/2 · n·R·T, and T here is the absolute temperature in kelvin. With n constant, U is proportional to T in kelvin.\nConvert: T_1 = 20 + 273 = 293 K, T_2 = 80 + 273 = 353 K.\nU_2/U_1 = T_2/T_1 = 353/293 ≈ 1.2\nThe internal energy grew by about 20 %, not four times. Mira is wrong: 80 is four times 20 only on the Celsius scale, whose zero is the freezing point of water — it is not the zero of molecular motion.",
