      statement: "A fixed amount of a monatomic ideal gas has a pressure of 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa and a volume of 3.0 × 10⁻³ m³. After a change of state its pressure is 6.0 × 10⁵ Pa and its volume is 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³.\n(a) Calculate the internal energy of the gas in the first state.\n(b) Determine the change in internal energy of the gas.\n(c) State what this tells you about the temperatures of the two states.",
      solution: "Nobody gave n or T — and they are not needed. For an ideal gas n·R·T = p·V, so U = 3/2 · n·R·T = 3/2 · p·V (B4.1, section 1).\n(a) p_1·V_1 = 4.0·10⁵ · 3.0·10⁻³ = 1200 J\nU_1 = 3/2 · p_1·V_1 = 1.5 · 1200 = 1800 J\n(b) p_2·V_2 = 6.0·10⁵ · 2.0·10⁻³ = 1200 J — the same product.\nΔU = 3/2 · (p_2·V_2 − p_1·V_1) = 1.5 · (1200 − 1200) = 0\n(c) U depends only on T, and U has not changed — so T_2 = T_1. The two states lie on the same isotherm.",
      answer: "(a) U_1 = 3/2 · p_1·V_1 = 1800 J\n(b) ΔU = 3/2 · (p_2·V_2 − p_1·V_1) = 0\n(c) T_2 = T_1 — the two states have the same temperature",
