      statement: "Steam in a cylinder pushes a piston out at a constant pressure of 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa. The volume of the steam increases from 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ to 5.0 × 10⁻³ m³.\n(a) Calculate the work done by the steam.\n(b) The piston is then pushed back until the volume is 4.0 × 10⁻³ m³, at the same pressure. State the work done by the steam in this step.",
      solution: "At constant pressure the work done by a gas is W = p·ΔV, where ΔV = final volume − initial volume (B4.1, section 2).\n(a) ΔV = V_2 − V_1 = 5.0·10⁻³ − 2.0·10⁻³ = 3.0·10⁻³ m³\nW = p·ΔV = 3.0·10⁵ · 3.0·10⁻³ = 900 J\nPositive: the steam expands and does work on the piston.\n(b) ΔV = V_3 − V_2 = 4.0·10⁻³ − 5.0·10⁻³ = −1.0·10⁻³ m³\nW = 3.0·10⁵ · (−1.0·10⁻³) = −300 J\nNegative: now the piston does work on the steam.",
      answer: "(a) W = p·ΔV = 900 J\n(b) W = −300 J (300 J of work is done on the steam)",
