      images: [], figure: "cards/figures/B4-P025.svg", keyInfo: "from state A to state B → direction A to B: V grows or falls?\nState whether work is done by the gas or on the gas → find: sign of W\nDetermine the work done → find W = area under AB (trapezium)",
      statement: "The graph shows how the pressure of a gas changes with its volume as the gas goes from state A to state B.\n(a) State whether work is done by the gas or on the gas.\n(b) Determine the work done.",
      solution: "(a) From A to B the volume increases — the gas expands. Work is done by the gas (W > 0).\n(b) The pressure is not constant, so W = p·ΔV cannot be used directly. The work is the area between the line AB and the V-axis (B4.1, section 3).\nThat area is a trapezium: its parallel sides are p_A and p_B, its width is ΔV = V_B − V_A.\nW = (p_A + p_B)/2 · ΔV\nFrom the graph: p_A = 4.0·10⁵ Pa, p_B = 2.0·10⁵ Pa, ΔV = 5.0·10⁻³ − 1.0·10⁻³ = 4.0·10⁻³ m³.\nW = (4.0·10⁵ + 2.0·10⁵)/2 · 4.0·10⁻³ = 3.0·10⁵ · 4.0·10⁻³ = 1200 J",
      answer: "(a) by the gas (the volume increases)\n(b) W = (p_A + p_B)/2 · ΔV = 1200 J",
      meaning: "For a straight line the work is \"average pressure × change in volume\". For a curve there is no such shortcut — you count squares.",
      whatIf: "What if the gas went back from B to A along the same line? The area is the same, but the volume now decreases: W = −1200 J. Out and back along one line gives zero net work — a cycle needs two different roads."
