      concept: "Constant temperature: ΔU = 0, so Q = W; why isothermal means slow",
      images: [], keyInfo: "temperature is the same at every depth → T = const: isothermal\nstays at the temperature of the water → ΔU = 0\nbubble expands → W > 0\ndoes 30 J of work → W = +30 J\nideal gas → U depends only on T\nState the change in internal energy of the air → find ΔU\nDetermine the thermal energy exchanged between the air and the water, → find Q = W\nstate its direction → into the bubble or out of it?\nSuggest why the bubble must rise slowly → explain: heat needs time to flow in",
      statement: "A diver breathes out, and an air bubble rises slowly through water whose temperature is the same at every depth. The air in the bubble stays at the temperature of the water. As it rises, the bubble expands and does 30 J of work on the water around it. Treat the air as an ideal gas.\n(a) State the change in internal energy of the air in the bubble.\n(b) Determine the thermal energy exchanged between the air and the water, and state its direction.\n(c) Suggest why the bubble must rise slowly for this to be true.",
      solution: "(a) The temperature of the air does not change, and the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature: ΔU = 0.\n(b) First law: Q = ΔU + W = 0 + 30 = +30 J. Positive Q — 30 J of thermal energy flows from the water into the air.\n(c) When the bubble expands it does work, and that energy has to come from somewhere. If heat is to keep the temperature constant, it needs time to flow in from the water. A slow rise gives it that time. A fast expansion would leave no time for heat to flow — the air would pay for the work with its own internal energy and cool down (that would be adiabatic).",
      answer: "(a) ΔU = 0 (constant temperature)\n(b) Q = W = +30 J, from the water into the air\n(c) heat needs time to flow in and keep T constant",
      meaning: "In an isothermal expansion the gas is a middleman: heat in, the same amount of work out, nothing kept. \"Isothermal\" never means \"no heat\" — it means exactly enough heat.",
