      anchors: ["A2.1#third-law-pairs"],
      buildsOn: ["A2-P001"], leadsTo: [],
      concept: "Third-law pair: same type of force, two different bodies, opposite directions",
      images: [], keyInfo: "A laptop rests on a desk → two bodies in contact, the Earth pulls both\nNewton's third law pair → same type of force, two bodies, A on B and B on A\nWhich row → check each row",
      statement: "A laptop rests on a desk.\nWhich row gives a correct Newton's third law pair of forces?\n\n| | Force A | Force B |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. | weight of the laptop | push of the laptop on the desk |\n| B. | gravitational pull of the Earth on the laptop | gravitational pull of the laptop on the Earth |\n| C. | weight of the laptop | push of the desk on the laptop |\n| D. | gravitational pull of the Earth on the laptop | gravitational pull of the desk on the Earth |",
      solution: "A third-law pair is always \"A pushes/pulls B — B pushes/pulls A\": the same type of force, acting on two different bodies.\nA — weight is a gravitational force, the push on the desk is a contact force: different types ✗.\nB — the Earth pulls the laptop, the laptop pulls the Earth: same type, two bodies ✓.\nC — both forces act on the laptop (they balance each other — that is the first law, not the third) ✗.\nD — three different bodies: Earth–laptop and desk–Earth ✗.",
