      statement: "(a) Two blocks of mass 3.0 kg and 5.0 kg are joined by a string and rest on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force F of 64 N is applied to the 5.0 kg block.\n[Figure 1]\nCalculate:\n(i) the acceleration of the blocks [1]\n(ii) the tension in the string. [1]\n(b) The 3.0 kg block is now placed on top of the 5.0 kg block. The coefficient of static friction between them is 0.50. Calculate the maximum horizontal force F that can be applied to the bottom block so that both blocks move together without slipping. [3]\n(c) The two blocks are now attached by a light inextensible string that passes over a smooth pulley. They are held stationary and suddenly released.\n[Figure 2]\nDetermine the acceleration of each block and the tension in the string. [4]\n(d) The string in part (c) suddenly snaps. Describe and explain the motion of the 3.0 kg block after that. (Assume it was moving upwards at the instant the string snapped.) [2]",
      solution: "(a)(i) a = F/(m₁ + m₂) = 64/8.0 = 8.0 m s⁻²\n(ii) The string gives the 3.0 kg block its acceleration: T = m₁·a = 3.0 · 8.0 = 24 N\n(b) Static friction accelerates the top block, at most μ_s·m₁g. Largest acceleration: a_max = μ_s·g = 0.50 · 9.81 ≈ 4.9 m s⁻²\nBoth blocks together: F_max = (m₁ + m₂)·a_max = 8.0 · 4.9 ≈ 39 N\n(c) The heavier block goes down. For the system: (m₂ − m₁)g = (m₁ + m₂)a → a = (5.0 − 3.0) · 9.81/8.0 ≈ 2.5 m s⁻² (3.0 kg up, 5.0 kg down)\nTension, from the 3.0 kg block: T − m₁g = m₁a → T = m₁(g + a) = 3.0 · (9.81 + 2.45) ≈ 37 N\n(d) Once the string snaps, only its weight acts: the block keeps moving up but slows down at g, stops for an instant, then falls back with acceleration g (free fall).",
      answer: "(a)(i) a = F/(m₁ + m₂) = 8.0 m s⁻² (ii) T = m₁a = 24 N\n(b) F_max = (m₁ + m₂)μ_s g ≈ 39 N\n(c) a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂) ≈ 2.5 m s⁻²; T = m₁(g + a) ≈ 37 N\n(d) rises while slowing at g, stops, then falls freely",
      meaning: "The same two blocks give three different answers: the string tension depends on which block is pulled, friction limits the acceleration, and over a pulley only the difference of the weights drives the motion.",
      whatIf: "In (a), what if the 64 N force pulled the 3.0 kg block instead? The acceleration is still 8.0 m s⁻², but now the string has to move the 5.0 kg block: T = 40 N."
