      statement: "(a) A space rocket is moving with constant velocity. Its engines are turned on and it accelerates by burning fuel and throwing out gases. Discuss how the law of conservation of momentum allows the rocket to accelerate forwards, although it throws gases out in the opposite direction. [3]\n(b) A rocket travels at constant velocity in space, far from any planet. The engines are turned off, and a module separates from the rocket.\n[Figure 1]\nThe module has a mass of 5000 kg and moves backwards at 8.0 km s⁻¹ after the separation. The combined mass of the rocket and the module is 60 000 kg, and the rest of the rocket travels forwards at 3000 m s⁻¹ after the separation. Calculate the initial speed of the rocket. [4]\n(c) The separation lasts 0.20 s. Calculate the average force exerted on the module during the separation. [3]\n(d) Inside the rocket, some walls are padded to reduce damage when the rocket accelerates. Explain, with reference to change in momentum, why padded walls are less likely to cause damage than rigid walls. [2]",
      solution: "(a) The rocket and the gases form one system with no external force, so their total momentum stays constant. The gases are pushed backwards and gain backward momentum; to keep the total the same, the rocket gains an equal forward momentum, so it speeds up. (Third law: the rocket pushes the gas back, the gas pushes the rocket forwards.)\n(b) Forwards positive. Total momentum before = total momentum after:\n60 000 · u = 55 000 · 3000 + 5000 · (−8000) = 1.65·10⁸ − 4.0·10⁷ = 1.25·10⁸\nu = 1.25·10⁸/60 000 ≈ 2.1·10³ m s⁻¹\n(c) Δp of the module = 5000 · (−8000 − 2083) ≈ −5.0·10⁷ kg m s⁻¹\nF = Δp/Δt = 5.0·10⁷/0.20 ≈ 2.5·10⁸ N (backwards)\n(d) An object hitting a wall has the same change of momentum whether the wall is padded or rigid. Padding makes the collision last longer, so F = Δp/Δt is smaller.",
      answer: "(a) total momentum conserved: gas gets backward momentum → rocket gets equal forward momentum\n(b) u ≈ 2.1·10³ m s⁻¹\n(c) F = Δp/Δt ≈ 2.5·10⁸ N\n(d) same Δp, longer Δt → smaller force",
