      images: [], keyInfo: "spherical hailstone of radius 2.5 mm → r (mm → m)\nat constant speed → terminal: drag = weight\nIgnore the buoyancy of the air → only W and drag\nDensity of ice = 920 kg m⁻³ → ρ_ice\nViscosity of air = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ Pa s → η\nweight of the hailstone → find W = ρ(4/3)πr³g\nUse Stokes' law → 6πηrv = W\nwithout any loss of mass → same weight\ndensity of water is 1000 kg m⁻³ → denser → smaller radius\nsmaller speed, the same speed or a higher speed → compare v = W/(6πηr)\nJustify → explain",
      statement: "During a thunderstorm, a spherical hailstone of radius 2.5 mm falls through still air at constant speed. Ignore the buoyancy of the air.\nDensity of ice = 920 kg m⁻³\nViscosity of air = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ Pa s\n(a) Calculate the weight of the hailstone. [1]\n(b) Use Stokes' law to calculate the speed at which the hailstone falls. [2]\n(c) Friction between the hailstone and the air makes the hailstone melt on its way down, without any loss of mass, turning it into a raindrop.\n(i) Given that the density of water is 1000 kg m⁻³, would you expect the raindrop to fall at a smaller speed, the same speed or a higher speed than the hailstone? [1]\n(ii) Justify your answer. [1]",
      solution: "(a) V = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)π · (2.5·10⁻³)³ ≈ 6.5·10⁻⁸ m³\nm = ρV = 920 · 6.5·10⁻⁸ ≈ 6.0·10⁻⁵ kg → W = mg ≈ 5.9·10⁻⁴ N\n(b) Constant speed: Stokes' drag = weight → 6πηrv = W\nv = W/(6πηr) = 5.9·10⁻⁴/(6π · 1.8·10⁻⁵ · 2.5·10⁻³) ≈ 7·10² m s⁻¹\n(c)(i) A higher speed.\n(ii) The mass, and so the weight, stays the same. Water is denser than ice, so the same mass takes a smaller volume: the raindrop has a smaller radius (r ∝ 1/ρ^(1/3), here about 3 % smaller). By Stokes' law v = W/(6πηr), a smaller r with the same W gives a larger terminal speed.",
      answer: "(a) W ≈ 5.9·10⁻⁴ N\n(b) v = W/(6πηr) ≈ 7·10² m s⁻¹\n(c)(i) higher (ii) same W, smaller r → larger v = W/(6πηr)",
