      concept: "Hovering drone: lift from pushing air down, (Δm/Δt)·v = total weight",
      images: [], figures: ["cards/figures/A2-P054_1.svg"], keyInfo: "mass 1.2 kg → m_d\nhovers at rest → F_res = 0\n0.40 kg parcel → m_p\n2.0 kg of still air vertically downwards every second → Δm/Δt, u = 0\nExplain, using Newton's third law → rotors push air down → air pushes rotors up\nspeed at which the air leaves → (Δm/Δt)·v = (m_d + m_p)·g\nparcel is released → lift unchanged, weight smaller\ninitial acceleration → a = (F_lift − m_d·g)/m_d",
      statement: "A delivery drone of mass 1.2 kg hovers at rest, carrying a 0.40 kg parcel hanging from a string. Its rotors push 2.0 kg of still air vertically downwards every second.\n[Figure 1]\n(a) State the resultant force on the drone with its parcel.\n(b) Explain, using Newton's third law, how the rotors produce an upward force.\n(c) Calculate the speed at which the air leaves the rotors.\n(d) The parcel is released. Calculate the initial acceleration of the drone.",
      solution: "(a) Zero — the drone is at rest.\n(b) The rotors push the air downward (they give it downward momentum). By the third law the air pushes the rotors upward with an equal force — the lift.\n(c) Lift = rate of change of momentum of the air = (Δm/Δt)·v. Hovering: lift = total weight = (1.2 + 0.40)·9.81 ≈ 15.7 N\nv = 15.7/2.0 ≈ 7.8 m s⁻¹\n(d) Just after release the lift is still 15.7 N, but only 1.2 kg is carried:\na = (15.7 − 1.2 · 9.81)/1.2 = (15.7 − 11.8)/1.2 ≈ 3.3 m s⁻² upward",
      answer: "(a) zero\n(b) rotors push air down → air pushes rotors up (third law)\n(c) v = (m_d + m_p)g/(Δm/Δt) ≈ 7.8 m s⁻¹\n(d) a = m_p·g/m_d ≈ 3.3 m s⁻² upward",
      meaning: "A drone does not \"hold on\" to the air — it constantly throws air down, like a rocket throws gas.",
      whatIf: "What if the drone must hover with a 1.0 kg parcel? It must give the air (2.2·9.81)/2.0 ≈ 10.8 m s⁻¹ (with the same mass flow) — the rotors must spin faster."
