      concept: "Pendulum at release: v = 0 → no centripetal force → T = mg cos θ",
      images: [], figures: ["cards/figures/A2-P059_1.svg"], keyInfo: "released from rest → v = 0 at that moment\nat an angle θ to the vertical → split mg along and across the rope\nWhat is the magnitude of the tension T → along the rope: T − mg cos θ = mv²/r = 0",
      statement: "A child sits on a playground swing. Together they have mass m, and the ropes can be treated as one string. The diagram shows the moment when the swing is released from rest with the ropes at an angle θ to the vertical.\n[Figure 1]\nWhat is the magnitude of the tension T in the ropes when the swing is released?\nA. mg sin(90° − θ)\nB. mg tan θ\nC. mg sin θ\nD. mg cos(90° − θ)",
      solution: "Along the rope (towards the pivot) the resultant force must be the centripetal force m·v²/r. At the moment of release v = 0, so nothing is needed towards the centre:\nT − mg·cos θ = 0 → T = mg·cos θ\ncos θ = sin(90° − θ), so this is option A.\nThe other component of the weight, mg·sin θ = mg·cos(90° − θ), is not balanced — it starts the swing moving along the arc. C and D are both this component, written in two ways.",
      answer: "A (T = mg cos θ = mg sin(90° − θ))",
