      statement: "The free-body diagram shows a sledge on a frictionless icy slope. The diagram and the forces are drawn to scale. The weight W and the normal force N are shown. The force F acts up the slope.\n[Figure 1]\nWhat is correct about the motion of the sledge?\nA. The sledge is sliding down the slope with a constant speed.\nB. The sledge is sliding down the slope with an increasing speed.\nC. The sledge is sliding up the slope with a constant speed.\nD. The sledge is sliding up the slope with an increasing speed.",
      solution: "Across the slope: N balances the component of W into the slope (the dashed line into the slope is as long as N).\nAlong the slope: the component of W down the slope (the dashed line along the slope) is clearly longer than F.\nSo the resultant force points down the slope → the acceleration points down the slope.\nA and C need a zero resultant. D needs the resultant up the slope.\nB: the sledge moves down the slope and speeds up — the velocity and the acceleration point the same way.\n(The diagram cannot exclude a sledge moving up and slowing down — but that option is not offered.)",
