      images: [], figures: ["cards/figures/A2-P070_1.svg"], keyInfo: "stationary ball → u = 0\nmass 0.43 kg → m\n21 m s⁻¹ → v\nAir resistance is negligible → a_x = 0, a_y = g in (a)–(b)\n50 ms → Δt (ms → s)\naverage force → find F = mv/Δt\nat an angle of 20° → v_x = v cos 20°, v_y = v sin 20°\n12 m → x to the wall\ntime taken for the ball to reach the wall → find t = x/v_x\n2.5 m above the ground → wall height\nDeduce whether the ball will hit the wall → compare y(t) with 2.5 m\nvertical acceleration → with drag: up and down separately\nrolls along the ground without sliding → v = ωr\n1.6 m s⁻¹ → v\n0.11 m → r\nangular velocity → find ω = v/r, with unit",
      statement: "A footballer kicks a stationary ball of mass 0.43 kg towards a wall. The initial speed of the ball after the kick is 21 m s⁻¹ and the ball does not rotate. Air resistance is negligible and there is no wind.\n[Figure 1]\n(a) The footballer's foot is in contact with the ball for 50 ms. Calculate the average force that acts on the ball due to the footballer. [2]\n(b) The ball leaves the ground at an angle of 20°. The horizontal distance from the ball to the wall is 12 m.\n(i) Calculate the time taken for the ball to reach the wall. [2]\n(ii) The top of the wall is 2.5 m above the ground. Deduce whether the ball will hit the wall. [3]\n(c) In practice, air resistance affects the ball. Outline the effect that air resistance has on the vertical acceleration of the ball. Take the direction of the acceleration due to gravity to be positive. [2]\n(d) The footballer kicks the ball again. It rolls along the ground without sliding with a horizontal velocity of 1.6 m s⁻¹. The radius of the ball is 0.11 m. Calculate the angular velocity of the ball. State an appropriate SI unit for your answer. [1]",
      solution: "(a) F = Δp/Δt = 0.43 · 21/0.050 ≈ 1.8·10² N\n(b)(i) v_x = 21 · cos 20° ≈ 19.7 m s⁻¹ → t = 12/19.7 ≈ 0.61 s\n(ii) v_y = 21 · sin 20° ≈ 7.18 m s⁻¹\ny = v_y·t − ½g·t² = 7.18 · 0.608 − 4.905 · 0.608² ≈ 4.37 − 1.81 ≈ 2.55 m\n2.55 m > 2.5 m → the ball just passes over the wall: it does not hit it.\n(c) On the way up drag points partly down, adding to gravity → the vertical acceleration is greater than g. On the way down drag points partly up → the vertical acceleration is less than g.\n(d) ω = v/r = 1.6/0.11 ≈ 15 rad s⁻¹",
      answer: "(a) F = mv/Δt ≈ 1.8·10² N\n(b)(i) t = x/(v cos 20°) ≈ 0.61 s (ii) y ≈ 2.55 m > 2.5 m → the ball just clears the wall, it does not hit it\n(c) up: greater than g; down: less than g\n(d) ω = v/r ≈ 15 rad s⁻¹",
      meaning: "One kick touches A.1 (projectile), A.2 (impulse, drag) and circular motion (rolling): exam questions like to tie topics together.",
      whatIf: "What if the wall were 20 m away? t ≈ 1.01 s, y ≈ 7.18·1.01 − 4.905·1.03 ≈ 2.2 m < 2.5 m — now the ball hits the wall, on its way down."
