      statement: "A crate is lifted by two cables. Each cable makes an angle θ with the vertical, and the tension in each cable has magnitude T.\n[Figure 1]\nThe crate moves vertically upwards with constant speed v. Air resistance is negligible.\nWhat is the rate of change of the gravitational potential energy of the crate?\nA. Tv sin θ\nB. Tv cos θ\nC. 2Tv sin θ\nD. 2Tv cos θ",
      solution: "Only the vertical components of the tensions do work, because the crate moves vertically. Each cable: T·cos θ upward; together 2T·cos θ.\nThe rate of work (power) = force along the motion × speed: P = 2T·cos θ·v.\nAt constant speed E_k does not change and there is no air resistance, so all of this work becomes gravitational potential energy.\n(Also: at constant speed 2T cos θ = mg, so the rate equals mgv.)\nThe horizontal components (T sin θ) cancel and do no work — A and C use them by mistake; A and B forget the second cable.",
